unemployment is rising as the economic  consequences of lockdown begin to bite  and today the Labour Party is warning  about a crunch date in less than three  weeks when mass redundancies could begin  well joining us from Westminster evening  shadow work and pensions secretary  Jonathan Rennell and thank you for being  on the program mr. Reynolds what exactly  is it that Labour is warning about good  morning sir the impact on unemployment  of this crisis really cannot be  overestimated we already have a claimant  count at 2.8 million we've had another  half a million universal credit  applications on top of that and we know  that young people only half of young  people who are eligible for out of work  benefits will claim them so it's  possible actually already that  unemployment is the highest that it's  ever been and the government cannot be  slow to act in response to that in  particular we need a jobs budget the  focus should be jobs jobs and jobs again  and crucially things like the furlough  scheme cannot be a one-size-fits-all  scheme if you look around the world a  lot of countries are acting flexibly we  need to see that in this country for  sectors that are still very much  affected by the crisis we also need to  see the government get directly involved  in the business of creating jobs  something like the Future Jobs Fund  which we saw after the financial crisis  the government guaranteeing people work  if they've been out of work for a  significant period of time that's the  kind of action that we need because we  know that the long-term effects of an  employment scar people for many years  after the experience of it itself I just  wonder if some of what you're calling  for the government is effectively  already doing you're saying they're  about a sector specific attitude towards  the furlough scheme but the Chancellor  merci Sudak said this week it's not  sustainable for the scheme to be  sustained indefinitely there are other  ways we can support the labor market  including direct fiscal support to those  particular industries most affected I  mean that sounds like the kind of  tailored support that you're calling for  and if you're asking for the government  to do something quickly well we're  expecting this speech now from the prime  minister next week where he's announcing  a building blitz to get the economy  restarted after coronavirus though isn't  what you're saying already in the  pipeline I'm afraid I don't think it is  I mean first of all on on build build  build we've heard things like that  before I mean I remember the march of  the makers 10 years ago what did we see  house building as its lowest of the last  decade since the second world war  capital investment in in Yorkshire in  the Midlands half of that of what it is  in London but  going back to the point around the  furlough scheme if the claimant council  already over three million and we have  over nine million people furloughed as  employers have to make contributions as  that part of the program starts the  danger is many of those people will  simply be made redundant if you look  around the world New Zealand has  extended its furlough scheme its wage  subsidy scheme to any business still  affected by a loss of revenue from the  crisis if you look at Spain or Italy  they're extending it to areas like  hospitality and tourism that are still  affected that's the kind of approach  that we need but it's only one part of  it alongside that bring forward  investment decisions make sure people  young people are kept in education  otherwise 800,000 young people will  enter the labor market this summer  that's worked in the past use the Future  Jobs Fund as a template for that just to  be clear then is labor calling for the  furlough scheme to be extended yes it  should be extended not in a universal  way but in a sector specific way to make  sure that as we continue through this  crisis we don't see those people  currently Furr Lord simply made  redundant and put further on to the  claimant camp okay now and you mentioned  about the issues around education is  something that we're going to be  exploring in the program later on I'm  visited a school this week where the  head teacher said that part of the issue  is parents are effectively too scared to  send their children back to school and  many pupils  he's worried it will be very frightened  when they do eventually return to the  classroom does labor need to take a bit  more responsibility for this it feels to  me like you've been quite slow in  calling for schools to reopen to more  pupils Kirsten mow hesitating and Prime  Minister's Questions to say that it's  safe to send children back should you  have done more to get schools reopened  no I don't think that's fair Sophie  everybody wants schools to go back the  impact on inequality of children being  off school for so long eyes I see and I  worry about it in terms of my own  children everybody should want schools  back but the only way to do that is to  convince parents that it's safe to work  with councils to work with Academy  trusts to work with the teaching unions  to find a way to do that only the  government can lead on that that's  pretty obvious but at the same time you  we have offered we suggested a task  force to get people to get this sorted  to make sure schools were safe to go  back and that people would feel that it  is safe to do so but the lack of  leadership from the government on this  real  has been a spectacular failure they've  got to take responsibility for it  they've been slow even to make sure that  people have the resources they require  to learn at home but no one should doubt  the impact of children not being at  school is incredibly severe on them but  the government has to lead on getting  them back so is it safe for children to  return to school now it will always be  about managing risks until we have a  vaccine it will always be about managing  risks in whatever sphere we are talking  about but there are ways the government  look at the approach for instance to the  the Nightingale hospitals that were set  up where's the equivalent approach in  education to that where's the  requisitioning of space to make sure we  can have bubble classrooms away from  traditional school buildings where's the  efforts to get resources to children at  home none of that has happened and the  responsibility for that is firmly at the  government's feet okay now you've got of  course a new shadow education secretary  in the party after Rebecca long Bailey  was sacked for tweeting a link to an  interview with the actress Maxine Peake  where she talked about what many would  consider to be an anti-semitic  conspiracy theory was Kirsten were right  to get rid of her well I have a lot of  time and respect for Becky and the job  that she was doing I want a broad base  shadow cabinet not because of party  unity by the way but because a strong  shadow cabinet reflects all the  viewpoints across yes the sharing of an  anti-semitic conspiracy theory I'm  afraid is not something that can be  ignored if people don't understand why  that was anti-semitic there's a lot of  resources available for people to  understand and find that out but we will  get no more chances we deserve no more  chances with the Jewish community to win  back the trust that has been lost and  Kia promised a zero tolerance I mean  that has to mean zero tolerance and I'm  afraid for that reason yes I'm afraid  that change had to be made actually  reflected slightly in your answer there  you know you want to work quite closely  with Rebecca long Bailey do you think  she has a blind spot on anti-semitism  where's me and this is a point more  generally I think that if anyone can't  understand what the problem is with an  article like that and I think it's  important to say you know who Maxine  Peake has apologised for for any  reference that was given within that  interview throughout time there has been  a strand of anti-semitism which has been  about blaming Jewish people for all the  problems in the world and a modern  incarnation of that  is simply replacing Jews with Israel now  that is something that's in the Labor  Party's own guidance that we send out on  anti-semitism and if someone is looking  at the terrible scenes in America and  somehow trying to change the blame for  that to Israel I mean the anti-semitic  warning lights should be flashing to  anyone who who sees that in print or  hears something like that so more  broadly people have to understand but  why this is such an issue and do you  think that Rebecca long Bailey perhaps  did not previously understand that I'm  not sure to be honest I when I saw the  article that had been shared I've got to  say I gasped I caught my breath I  assumed perhaps it hadn't been read in  an apology week forthcoming I don't know  exactly you know why that is still a  player on Twitter but you know there's  no doubt it is an anti-semitic  conspiracy theory and we promise zero  tolerance and that has to be what we we  practice and preach okay now I just want  to talk about your brief as well as a  the shadow work and Pensions Secretary  now you've called for universal credit  to be scrapped given it has taken so  long to set up millions of people now  rely on it is it sensible to be calling  for an overhaul of the system at a time  when it's already under so much pressure  because of Coppa 19 I'm afraid I think  it is what you need from universal  credit or whatever replaces it is a  safety net a strong safety net that gets  people through crises like this one but  also times when they've lost their job  or they're too ill to work and that is  not what Universal Credit provides I  mean the figures out last week nearly  three and a half million British people  dependent on food charity or food banks  to get by as six million British people  having to have smaller portions for  their meals but they can't manage with  what they receive I want to stress as  well so we said during the crisis that  the changes we want to see the  government make are actually practical  changes to universal credit things like  scrubbing the two-child limit and the  benefit cap and crucially the five-week  wait so we've been pragmatic in what we  have asked for but going forward if we  need the kind of Social Security system  that we do need just to cope with the  changes of the impacts of technology on  people's jobs or or just a normal  situation where people get ill at times  and they need support from the system  Universal Credit just does not provide  that and at the same time I think the  brand the approach of universal credit  that very punitive sanctions based  approach that is not the right way  forward  one in their lives at some point may  need support from a social security  system and that is simply not what they  get from the universal credit I'm  interested to know about your own  intentions for the benefit system as  well because a few weeks ago you said  this I feel if you've made greater  contributions to the system there is an  argument that you should receive more  out of the system and kissed Armour when  he was the Director of Public  Prosecutions he actually increased jail  sentences the benefit sheet saying it's  vital that we take a tough stance on  this type of fraud and I'm determined to  see a clamp down on those who flout the  system so under a kiss Thomas leadership  here in you in the job is labor about to  get tough on benefits no I regret I do  not accept at all the kind of rhetoric  we once saw from people like George  Osborne about trying to be divisive in  terms of the Social Security system I  want to be absolutely clear what I'm  talking about  contributory benefits are a crucial part  of the existing system if you apply for  universal credit and you don't get it  because it's a means-tested benefit what  you will get instead if you paid your  National Insurance Contributions our  Jobseeker's Allowance that the national  insurance based benefits that we have in  the system now for the first time  because the government has failed to  operate those legacy benefits alongside  the increase in Universal Credit the  National Insurance level of support is  lower than the means-tested level of  support now that is completely  unacceptable 74 pounds a week is a not  sufficient compensation for the National  Insurance Contributions people have paid  so that is what I am talking about the  government's answer to that as you know  is simply to say and we can't upgrade  the system the computer says no that is  completely unacceptable this is not a  small part of the system hundreds of  thousands of people are dependent on  this aspect though because they don't  qualify for universal credit and they're  not getting what they need and they're  not getting what they deserve and when  we talk about finding a replacement for  universal credit we've got to consider  this respect of it as well because  people as you know are currently  eligible for both it's just if you claim  universal credit it will take away every  pound that you would get from a  contributory benefit so most people  don't claim both at the same time

